DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/comments/{{createCommentDay12.response.body.$.id}}

### Check that the comment list is now empty
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/{{createArticleDay12.response.body.$.id}}/comments

### ==========================================================
### DAY 13: search/sort/pagination on GET /api/articles.
### The results depend on the articles already present.
### ==========================================================

### Default listing (page 1, 10 per page, most recent first)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/explore

### Search (adapt the term to the title of an existing published article)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/explore?search=article

### Sort from oldest to most recent
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/explore?sort=oldest

### Pagination: 2 per page, page 1
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/explore?limit=2&page=1

### Pagination: 2 per page, page 2 (results should differ from page 1)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/explore?limit=2&page=2

### Guard rails: negative page -> brought back to 1, no error
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/explore?page=-5

### Guard rails: huge limit -> capped at 50, no error
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/explore?limit=99999

### ==========================================================
### DAY 14: Redis cache on the article listing. The content of the
### HTTP response is the same whether the data comes from the cache or from the
### database. To really check that it works, connect to redis-cli:
### docker compose -f platform/docker-compose.yml exec redis redis-cli KEYS "articles:*"
### -> a GET request must create a key "articles:list:v0:...".
### -> creating/updating/deleting an article must move
###    "articles:cache:version" from empty/0 to 1, and the next GET request
###    must create a new key "articles:list:v1:...".
### ==========================================================

### Fill the cache (v0)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/articles/explore

